Subject: Bayesian normal models with random effects
In-Reply-To: <alpine.OSX.2.00.0912180947360.46368@pol-g88381xtxyl.princeton.edu>
References: <E44AB516-CEF2-4E23-AD8A-D38E1D424864@gmail.com> <alpine.OSX.2.00.0912180947360.46368@pol-g88381xtxyl.princeton.edu>
Message-ID: <6B810AFB14C606439FD57E5985E037910477C37A@useagan1500p.GLOBAL.ECOLAB.CORP>

Hello,

Is it possible to create normal.bayes models in Zelig that have random effects?  Here's my application:  I would like to create specifications for an electrical motor on how much electrical current they draw.  Eighty samples from five batches of motor were measured, with different numbers of motors from each batch. So the batches are a random effect.

I've been advised that a Bayesian analysis will handle the unbalanced data better. Batches 1 & 2 had 20 motors measured apiece, Batch 3 had 15 motors, Batch 4 had 4 motors, leaving the last motor from the 5th batch.   

My objective is to get an interval that will capture a tolerance range of the current measurements.  Let's say an interval that predicts 99% of future Current Draw measurements.  Does Zelig have this capability, I didn't find it in the few vignettes I read through.  

Regards, Paul

