References: <E44AB516-CEF2-4E23-AD8A-D38E1D424864@gmail.com> <alpine.OSX.2.00.0912180947360.46368@pol-g88381xtxyl.princeton.edu> <6B810AFB14C606439FD57E5985E037910477C37A@useagan1500p.GLOBAL.ECOLAB.CORP>
Message-ID: <b20ae6290912211334g4987362cud1e61c9f1de53ead@mail.gmail.com>

Paul,

you can fit so called mixed effect models with Zelig via lme4 package.
However, that is not full Bayesian analysis, as realised values of random
effects are evaluated using the mode of posterior for variance components
instead of full distribution, i.e., REML analysis. But, this is a first and
easy step in the direction you want to go.

gg

